    "lawCode": "Amd §§1604, 1709, 1804, 1950, 2503, 2554 & 2854, Ed L; amd §12, Chap 147 of 2001",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8564\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn  act to amend the education law, in relation to limiting the duration\nof a conditional appointment and to amend chapter 147  of  the  laws  of\n2001,  amending the education law relating to conditional appointment of\nschool district, charter school  or  BOCES  employees,  in  relation  to\nextending the effectiveness thereof\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\nTo  extend  the ability of school districts, charter schools, and Boards\nof Cooperative Educational Services (BOCES) to make conditional appoint-\nments of prospective employees under certain necessary conditions and to\nlimit the duration of a conditional appointment.\n\nSummary of provisions:  Section 1 of the bill would amend paragraph  (b)\nof  subdivision  39 of section 1604 of the Education Law to provide that\nthe conditional clearance for prospective  employees  of  common  school\ndistricts  would  terminate  forty-five  days  after notification by the\nCommissioner of such conditional clearance and could not be extended  or\nrenewed  unless  a  new  conditional clearance is issued after a finding\nthat the employee demonstrated good cause for failing to  obtain  clear-\nance within the initial forty-five day period.\n\n§2  of  the  bill would amend paragraph (b) of subdivision 39 of section\n1709 of the Education Law to permit prospective  employees  of  a  union\nfree  school  district to be issued a conditional clearance, which would\nterminate forty-five days after notification by the Commissioner of such\nconditional clearance. Such conditional clearance could not be  extended\nor  renewed after the initial forty-five day period unless the applicant\nhad demonstrated good cause for failing to obtain clearance  within  the\ninitial thirty day period.\n\n§3  of  the  bill  would amend paragraph (b) of subdivision 9 of section\n1804 of the Education Law to permit potential  employees  of  a  central\nschool district to be issued a conditional clearance, which would termi-\nnate  forty-five  days  after  notification  by the Commissioner of such\nconditional clearance. Such conditional clearance could not be  extended\nor  renewed after the initial forty-five day period unless the applicant\nhad demonstrated good cause for failing to obtain clearance  within  the\ninitial thirty day period.\n\n§4  of the bill would amend subparagraph (b) of paragraph 11 of subdivi-\nsion 4 of section 1950 of the Education  Law  to  permit  a  prospective\nemployee  of  a  BOCES to be issued a conditional clearance, which would\nterminate forty-five days after notification by the Commissioner of such\nconditional clearance. Such conditional clearance could not be  extended\nor  renewed after the initial forty-five day period unless the applicant\nhad demonstrated good cause for failing to obtain clearance  within  the\ninitial thirty day period.\n\n§5  of  the  bill would amend paragraph (b) of subdivision 18 of section\n2503 of the Education Law to permit a prospective employee  of  a  small\ncity  school  district to be issued a conditional clearance, which would\nterminate forty-five days after notification by the Commissioner of such\nconditional  clearance. Such conditional clearance could not be extended\nor renewed after the initial forty-five day period unless the  applicant\nhad  demonstrated  good cause for failing to obtain clearance within the\ninitial thirty day period.\n\n§6 of the bill would amend paragraph (b) of subdivision  25  of  section\n2554  of  the  Education Law to permit a prospective employee of a large\ncity school district (other than the  New  York  City  school  district,\nwhich  conducts  its  own criminal history record checks) to be issued a\nconditional clearance,  which  would  terminate  forty-five  days  after\nnotification  by  the  Commissioner  of such conditional clearance. Such\nconditional clearance could not be extended or renewed after the initial\nforty-five day period unless the applicant had demonstrated  good  cause\nfor failing to obtain clearance within the initial thirty day period.\n\n§7  of  the  bill  would  amend  subparagraph (ii) of paragraph (a-2) of\nsubdivision 3 of section 2854 of the Education Law to permit a  prospec-\ntive  employee of a charter school to be issued a conditional clearance,\nwhich would terminate forty-five days after notification by the  Commis-\nsioner  of  such conditional clearance. Such conditional clearance could\nnot be extended or renewed  after  the  initial  forty-five  day  period\nunless  the  applicant had demonstrated good cause for failing to obtain\nclearance within the initial thirty day period.\n\n§8 of the bill would extend the date on which the provisions of  Chapter\n147 of the Laws of 2001 would expire from July 1, 2009 to July 1, 2010.\n\n§9  of  the bill would provide that all conditional clearances issued by\nthe Commissioner of Education pursuant to  sections  1604,  1709,  1804,\n1950,  2503,  2554  or  2854 of the Education Law prior to the effective\ndate of this bill would expire forty-five days after the effective  date\nof  July 1, 2009. Such conditional appointments would not be extended or\nrenewed unless the applicant demonstrates  good  cause  for  failing  to\nobtain clearance within this forty-five day period.\n\n§ 10 of the bill would be the effective date.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT OF THE BILL:\nChapter  147  of  the  Laws  of  2001  granted school districts, charter\nschools, and BOCES the authority to make  conditional  appointments  for\ncertain  prospective  school  employees.  This was needed because it was\ntaking a longer than expected time to secure full clearances for employ-\nment from the Commissioner of Education under  Education  Law  §305(30).\nChapter 147 of the Laws of 2001 provided that it would be repealed after\ntwo  years,  on  the assumption that these delays would be minimized and\neliminated. Both Chapters 127 of the Laws of 2005 and Chapter 90 of  the\nLaws of 2007 extended the effectiveness of Chapter 147 for an additional\ntwo years due to the continued need for conditional clearances.\n\nDespite  the  introduction  of a new interactive database (TEACH) in the\nState Education Department (SED) to provide better information to school\ndistricts, BOCES, charter schools and individual  applicants  about  the\nstatus of an individual's fingerprint application and the use of finger-\nprint card scanning technology, SED continues to have a backlog of fing-\nerprint  applications.  SED is implementing new technology (LIVESCAN) to\ncapture digital fingerprint images  in  the  field.  This  will  greatly\nreduce  the  time  between  when  fingerprints are submitted to when the\nclearance for employment is issued. SED anticipates that  LIVESCAN  will\nbe  increasingly  available  to  school  districts,  charter schools and\nBOCES. The use of LIVESCAN in conjunction with TEACH should allow  fing-\nerprint  clearance  results  to be available within a few days following\nthe transmission of the digital fingerprint images, in  most  instances.\nThere  will,  however,  always  be  situations  in  which clearances for\nemployment cannot be granted quickly. For example, the results of  Divi-\nsion  of Criminal Justice Services (DCJS) and Federal Bureau of Investi-\ngation (FBI) searches are sometimes delayed, a situation totally  beyond\nSED's  control. Or, certain digital fingerprint images may contain flaws\nand errors that require the applicant  to  be  re-fingerprinted.    Such\n\"rejections\"  of fingerprint images cause additional delays in issuing a\nclearance for employment. In addition, some individuals  will  not  have\naccess  to  a  digital  fingerprint scan station due to their geographic\nlocation or an employer's decision to  continue  submitting  fingerprint\ncards  rather  than digital fingerprint images.  Thus, the authority for\nschool  districts,  charter  schools,  and  BOCES  to  make  conditional\nappointments  is  still  necessary so they can meet employment needs and\nthe needs of their students.\n\nIf Chapter 147 were to sunset,  the  technical  amendments  relating  to\nteacher  removal proceedings that were made by section 11 of Chapter 147\nwould also sunset. These amendments are necessary to make the timeframes\nfor conducting informal conferences with teachers and  building  princi-\npals  following  a  teacher's  removal  of a student workable for school\ndistricts. The amendments made by section 11 of Chapter 147 clarify that\nif the 24- or 48-hour period does not expire on  a  school  day,  it  is\nextended  to  the  corresponding  time  on the next school day. If these\nprovisions are not extended, a teacher  would  be  required  to  provide\nparents  with  an  informal opportunity to be heard within 24 hours of a\npupil's removal, even if the 24 hours expires on a  Saturday  or  school\nholiday.  Similarly,  building  principals  would  be  required to offer\nparents the opportunity for an informal conference within 48 hours of  a\nstudent's  removal  even  if the student is removed late in the day on a\nFriday or before a school vacation.\n\nFurther, this bill would provide that conditional  clearances  would  be\nvalid  for  forty-five days. Under present law there is no time limit on\nan issued conditional  clearance.  In  February  2009  the  State  Comp-\ntroller's  Office  issued an audit of Criminal History Background Checks\nfor School Employees, Report 207-S-119, in which the  State  Comptroller\nidentified a substantial number of instances in which a background check\nhad  not  been completed (469 out of 105,932 applications then pending).\nOf  that  number,  427  of  the  criminal  history  record  checks  were\n\ncompleted,  but  the  State Comptroller found that the average length of\ntime between the completion of the DCJS background check and  the  final\nFBI check for these delayed applications was at least eight months. This\nposes  an  unacceptable  and  extended  risk  of  students'  exposure to\nprospective employees who may have the types of criminal histories  that\ncould  result in their preclusion from such employment. To address this,\nthe bill would establish a time limit for conditional clearances. Condi-\ntional clearances were never intended to allow prospective employees  to\ncontinue to be employed for extended periods without completing both the\nstate DCJS and FBI criminal history record checks.\n\nIt is apparent from this data that there are too many instances in which\nschool  districts,  BOCES  and  charter schools are allowing prospective\nemployees  to  continue  employment  for  a  protracted  period  without\ncompleting  a  full  criminal history record check. There can be signif-\nicant delays in the processing of fingerprints through  the  FBI,  which\nhas  rigorous standards that cause more rejections of fingerprint images\nthan at the state level, but delays of this magnitude should  not  occur\nwith  this  frequency.  To  address  this problem and reduce the risk to\nstudents from contact  with  prospective  employees  who  have  not  yet\nobtained  criminal clearance from the FBI's national registry, this bill\nwould impose a forty-five day maximum duration for a conditional  clear-\nance  and  prohibit  renewal  or  extension of a conditional appointment\nunless the Commissioner issues a new conditional clearance upon a  show-\ning  of  good  cause  for  failing  to  obtain full clearance within the\ninitial forty-five days. For any prospective employee who holds a condi-\ntional clearance on the effective date of  this  act,  this  bill  would\nprovide  that  the  conditional  clearance  would expire forty-five days\nafter the effective date and could not be extended or renewed without  a\nshowing  of good cause for the delay. Thus, delays in processing finger-\nprints attributable to lack of diligence  on  the  part  of  prospective\nemployers  or  their  employees  would not be excused, but in those rare\ninstances in which the delay is attributable to processing delays at the\nstate or federal levels, the bill would provide a mechanism for allowing\nthe prospective employee to continue their employment beyond  forty-five\ndays. The vast majority of criminal history record checks would be proc-\nessed  much more quickly, but in the few instances of significant delays\nin the processing of fingerprints through the FBI, there wo\n",
